CH = dict(num=9, title="Methods of Separation in Everyday Life", theme="Handpicking, threshing, winnowing, sieving, evaporation, filtration, magnetic separation",
 intro="Malli and Valli's 'Bharat ki Yatra' across India shows how everyday life depends on **separating mixtures**: grains from stones, husk from grain, bran from flour, salt from seawater, tea leaves from tea, butter from curd and iron nails from sawdust. Kabir's couplet compares winnowing — husk blown away, grain retained — to keeping virtues and discarding vices.",
 big_questions=["Why do we separate substances from a mixture?", "Which method suits which kind of mixture?", "How can a solid dissolved in a liquid be recovered?", "How are mixtures with many components separated?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "9.1 Why Do We Separate Substances?"),
 ("p", "We separate to **remove unwanted/harmful components** (stones from grain, tea leaves from tea) and to **obtain a useful component** (butter from curd, salt from seawater). The right method depends on the **properties of the components** — size, weight, solubility, magnetism, etc."),
 ("h2", "9.2 Methods of Separation"),
 ("h3", "(a) Handpicking"),
 ("p", "Picking particles by hand from a mixture on the basis of differences in **size, colour and shape** (stones and husk from wheat or rice; black pepper from pulao). Convenient when the quantity to be removed is **small** and easily picked."),
 ("h3", "(b) Threshing"),
 ("p", "Separating **grains from harvested stalks** by beating them (on a wooden log); mechanical **threshers** do threshing and winnowing together."),
 ("h3", "(c) Winnowing"),
 ("p", "Separating **heavier and lighter components** using wind or blown air. Farmers pour threshed grain from a raised platform in the direction of the wind, using a **soop (bamboo tray)**: heavy grains fall near, lighter husk blows away. Similarly, blowing on rubbed roasted peanuts removes the light skins."),
 ("h3", "(d) Sieving"),
 ("p", "Used when components of a **solid–solid mixture differ in size**. Fine flour passes through holes; bran and small stones stay on the sieve. The holes must be smaller than the particles to be retained and larger than those to pass. Also used at construction sites to separate pebbles/stones from sand."),
 ("h3", "(e) Evaporation"),
 ("ul", ["Used to recover a **solid dissolved in a liquid**. Water evaporates, leaving solid behind.", "**Salt from seawater:** seawater is kept in shallow pits in sunlight and air; water evaporates over days leaving a solid mixture, from which common salt is purified. Sources: seawater, **Sambhar Lake (Rajasthan)**. Namak Satyagraha (**Dandi March**) is associated with Sabarmati Ashram, Ahmedabad.", "Activity 9.2: drops of salt solution on dark paper dry, leaving white salt patches (like white patches on clothes after sweating). Activity 9.3 (teacher demo): heating salt solution in a **china dish** leaves salt behind.", "Ayurvedic herbs are shade-dried so excess water evaporates, leaving the medicinal parts.", "Evaporation alone does not give back the water; to recover both salt and water, evaporated water vapour must be **condensed**."]),
 ("h3", "(f) Sedimentation and decantation"),
 ("table", ["Term", "Meaning", "Example"],
  [["**Sedimentation**", "Settling of heavier insoluble component at the bottom of a liquid", "Tea leaves settle in a pan; mud in water; rice and pulses before washing"],
   ["**Decantation**", "Removing the liquid by gently tilting the vessel, leaving the sediment", "Pouring tea off leaves; draining water from washed rice; oil from water"]]),
 ("p", "Decantation does **not completely** separate (a few tea leaves may pour out), so it is a rough method. Oil and water separate into layers on standing, then can be decanted."),
 ("h3", "(g) Filtration"),
 ("p", "Separating **insoluble solid particles from a liquid** by passing the mixture through a **filter** that has pores. Tea strainer for tea leaves; cloth pores (used since ancient times) for muddy water; **filter paper** (finest pores) folded into a cone in a funnel over a conical flask (Activity 9.4)."),
 ("table", ["Term", "Meaning"],
  [["**Residue**", "Solid left on the filter paper (mud)"], ["**Filtrate**", "Clear liquid collected in the flask"]]),
 ("ul", ["Cotton, charcoal and sand can also act as filters; the choice depends on the **size of particles** to be removed.", "Tea bags were first of silk, then gauze/muslin, now filter paper.", "A fisherman's **net** acts like a filter — water drains through the mesh while fish are retained (but also plastic waste, showing river pollution: stop plastic at source).", "Nasal hair and mask material also filter dust."]),
 ("h3", "(h) Churning"),
 ("p", "Curd or milk is churned with a **mathni (churner)** or machine; lighter **butter floats on top** and buttermilk (chhach) remains. Used to extract butter."),
 ("h3", "(i) Magnetic separation"),
 ("p", "Separating **magnetic from non-magnetic substances with a magnet** — iron nails from sawdust; recyclers separate scrap iron from waste using magnets fitted on cranes."),
 ("h2", "9.3 Summary Table"),
 ("table", ["Mixture", "Method", "Property used"],
  [["Stones in pulses; black pepper in pulao", "Handpicking", "Size, colour, shape"],
   ["Grains and stalks", "Threshing", "Beating stalks"],
   ["Husk and grains", "Winnowing", "Weight (wind)"],
   ["Flour and bran", "Sieving", "Particle size"],
   ["Salt from salt solution/seawater", "Evaporation", "Water evaporates, salt does not"],
   ["Heavy insoluble solid and liquid", "Sedimentation + decantation", "Settles down"],
   ["Muddy water; tea leaves", "Filtration", "Insoluble particles larger than pores"],
   ["Butter from curd", "Churning", "Lighter butter floats"],
   ["Iron nails and sawdust", "Magnetic separation", "Magnetic property"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "A mixture with **more than two components needs a combination of methods**. Example: iron nails, sand, pepper, stones, salt and water — first magnet (nails), handpick pepper/stones, then add water to dissolve salt, filter off sand, and evaporate to get salt. Plan the sequence from the property that is easiest to use."),
 ("h2", "9.4 Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [("Why do we separate components of a mixture?", "To remove unwanted components or to obtain useful ones."),
  ("Define handpicking. When is it suitable?", "Picking by hand on basis of size, colour, shape; when impurity quantity is small."),
  ("Differentiate threshing and winnowing.", "Threshing separates grain from stalks by beating; winnowing separates husk from grains using wind."),
  ("What is sieving?", "Separating solid components by particle size using a sieve."),
  ("How is salt obtained from seawater?", "Seawater is evaporated in shallow pits by sunlight, then the salt is purified."),
  ("What are sedimentation and decantation?", "Settling of heavier insoluble solid; pouring off the liquid by tilting."),
  ("Why is decantation not a proper method of separation?", "It cannot remove all the particles."),
  ("What is filtration? Define residue and filtrate.", "Separating insoluble solid from liquid with a filter; solid left is residue and the liquid collected is filtrate."),
  ("Which method separates butter from curd?", "Churning."),
  ("How can iron nails be separated from sawdust?", "By magnetic separation with a magnet."),
  ("Which method is used to separate oil from water?", "Decantation (after layers form)."),
  ("Outline separating potato, salt and sawdust.", "Handpick potatoes; add water, sawdust floats/skim or filter; filter and evaporate the filtrate to get salt.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Separation removes unwanted or obtains useful components; method depends on property differences.",
  "Handpicking (size/colour), threshing, winnowing (weight), sieving (size).",
  "Evaporation recovers dissolved solids; sedimentation + decantation and filtration separate insoluble solids from liquids.",
  "Churning separates butter; magnets separate iron.",
  "Combine several methods for multi-component mixtures."])
